It all started as an experiment. Keith, whilst aimlessly searching the
Internet looking for inspiration for a generative art project, came across some
nice random graphics using Perlin noise. Delving further he found that the
creator of Perlin noise, Ken Perlin, was the recipient of an Oscar in 1996 for
the development of Perlin noise, a technique used to produce natural appearing
textures on computer generated surfaces for motion picture visual
effects. Perlin
originally developed the idea of pseudo-random noise whilst working on the
computer graphics for the film {\em Tron} in 1982.

This became the seed for thinking about creating interesting pseudo-random
graphics using Perlin noise in \METAFUN, the graphical subsystem that \CONTEXT\
provides. Here we will follow the steps that brought us native noise generation
in \CONTEXT. It is a story about experimenting, getting hooked, exploring
performance gain and eventually interfacing.
